C/ Verifies a VECTOR to-partition subscript (`V(4 TO 7)`) works as an
C/ assignment receiver, not just a readable value -- `V(4 TO 7) = SUBV;`
C/ (SUBV itself a VECTOR(4)) writes straight back into V's own storage.
C/ A plain SCALAR RHS (`V(4 TO 7) = 5.0;`) is illegal HAL/S (confirmed
C/ via a direct HALSFC compile: "TYPE OF V IS ILLEGAL FOR ASSIGNMENT
C/ FROM GIVEN RIGHT-HAND SIDE"), so this is only ever reached with a
C/ genuine VECTOR-shaped source.

  VTOPART: PROGRAM;
     DECLARE V VECTOR(10);
     DECLARE SUBV VECTOR(4) INITIAL(5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0);
M    V         = SUBV;
S      4 TO 7
     WRITE(6) V;
Y %SVCI(0);
  CLOSE VTOPART;
